      "claim": "C83 reports that pirazolac, an acidic 1-aryl-3,5-diaryl pyrazole carboxylate sharing the query's azole-carboxylate-diaryl framework, had complete systemic availability in humans (oral/IV AUC ratio f = 0.88–0.99), directly supporting F >= 20% for this structural class.",
      "card_ids": ["C83"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4 contains no query-specific intestinal permeability or solubility measurement; the PAMPA low-permeability result for salicylic acid (C115) is not transferable because the query is not salicylic acid and PAMPA is an artificial membrane, while azole-containing PAMPA records (C111, C125) show moderate-to-good permeability, consistent with absorption.",
      "card_ids": ["C111", "C115", "C125"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Solubility and dissolution records for unrelated molecules (e.g., diacerein, bendazac, tryptanthrin, indoprofen) are neutral for the query and do not provide affirmative evidence for F < 20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C116", "C139", "C144", "C118"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "intestinal_absorption_permeability",
      "applicability": "low",
      "direction": "neutral_or_unclear",
      "decision_effect": "no_change",
      "card_ids": ["C111", "C115", "C125", "C126"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct oral bioavailability measurement for the query molecule.",
    "No query-specific intestinal permeability or solubility data in L4; most records are for unrelated scaffolds.",
    "Transferability of the pirazolac high-F result is limited by structural differences (pyrazole vs triazole, halogens vs ortho-phenols, acetic acid vs benzoic acid).",
    "No query-specific metabolism or first-pass data.",
    "PAMPA records are artificial-membrane assays and do not capture active transport or paracellular absorption."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "L4 adds no query-specific intestinal absorption, solubility, or permeability measurement; the salicylic acid PAMPA low-permeability result is not transferable, and azole-containing PAMPA records are weakly supportive. The direct high-F comparator pirazolac (C83) remains the strongest evidence, and no affirmative low-F evidence appears. Prediction remains high with moderate confidence."
}

